# Dynamic Array

Implementation of a dynamic/resizable array in Python.

## Approach

The array keeps track of two values:

- `size`: number of elements currently stored
- `capacity`: total number of elements that can be stored before resizing

When `pushback()` is called and the array is full, the capacity is doubled.
A new array is allocated and the existing elements are copied to it.

## Operations

| Operation | Complexity |
|---|---:|
| get | O(1) |
| set | O(1) |
| pushback | O(1) amortized |
| popback | O(1) |
| resize | O(n) |
| getSize | O(1) |
| getCapacity | O(1) |

## Key Takeaway

Dynamic arrays provide constant-time random access while allowing the
underlying storage to grow when necessary.

Doubling the capacity during resizing makes `pushback()` O(1) amortized,
even though an individual resize operation takes O(n).